Abstract
In this study, a sulfate radical-advanced oxidation process (SR-AOP) was proposed by utilizing a bimetallic Co/Fe metal-organic frameworks/cellulose nanofiber membrane (Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF) as a catalyst for TBBPA degradation. Sulfate radicals $(\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{\cdot-})$ and hydroxyl radicals (OH·) were generated through the activation of peroxymonosulfate (PMS) by Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF. Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF was prepared by a facile solvothermal method and vacuum filtration. CNF acted as a natural substrates material to relieve the agglomeration of loaded MOFs. Additionally the composite membranes was easily separated from the reaction solution. The properties of the composite materials and the main factors that influenced TBBPA degradation were elucidated in detail, along with the TBBPA degradation intermediates, recyclability, and TBBPA degradation pathway. Almost $100\%$ of TBBPA was degraded within $30\mathrm{min}$ under optimal conditions , and the rate constant was determined to be $0.764\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ . Furthermore, the degradation rate of the composite membrane was $60\%$ after 4 cycles.
Keyword: Bimetallic Metal-organic frameworks; SR-AOPs; CNF; Heterogeneous catalysis; Tetrabromobisphenol A
## 1. Introduction
After decades of study, many methods have been developed to remove TBBPA from environmental media such as adsorption (Gu, Lou, Tian, Liu, & Tang, 2016), microbial degradation (Shen et al., 2018), photocatalytic degradation (Xu, Meng, Zhang, Li, & Guo, 2011), electrochemical degradation(Y. Zhang et al., 2019), Fenton/Fenton-like degradation (He, Ji, Wang, & Zhang, 2017) and sulfate radical-advanced oxidation processes (SR-AOPs) (Peng, Tian, Liu, & Jia, 2017). Among these methods, SR-AOPs is a relatively effective technology to completely degrade biorecalcitrant organic compounds (Nidheesh, Gandhimathi, & Ramesh, 2013). In the SR-AOP, sulfate radicals $(\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{\cdot-})$ play an important role in eliminating organic pollutants. SO4·– can be produced by activating peroxymonosulfate (PMS) or persulfate (PS) with heat (Yuefei Ji, Dong, Kong, Lu, & Zhou, 2015), ultraviolet light (Khan, He, Khan, Shah, & Dionysiou, 2013), transition metals (ANIPSITAKIS & DIONYSIOU, 2004) or ultrasonic waves (Cai, Zhang, Zhong, & Hou, 2015). The standard reduction potential $(\mathrm{E}^{0})$ of $\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{\cdot-}$ (2.5-3.1 V) is comparable to hydroxyl radicals (OH·, 1.8-2.7 V) which are produced by Fenton or Fenton-like processes due to their strong oxidation ability. Moreover, $\mathrm{SO}_{4}\mathrm{^{-}}$ 1 $(\mathrm{t}1/2)~=~30{-}40~\upmu\mathrm{s})$ has a longer half-life than OH· $(\mathrm{t}1/2\leq1\ \upmu\mathrm{s})$ , and it has been verified that the activity of $\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{\cdot\cdot}$ is independent of $\mathrm{pH}$ , while the activity of OH· decreases with increasing $\mathrm{pH}$ value (Oh, Dong, & Lim, 2016). Given these advantages, SR-AOPs have become an indispensable technology for water purification. For example, Ji et al. reported the oxidation of TBBPA by $\mathrm{Co(II)}$ catalyzed PMS decomposition (Y. Ji et al., 2016). Nevertheless, the application of homogeneous catalysts are limited by the difficult recovery, and the high quantity of residual in water (Ghanbari & Moradi, 2017). Many papers have used heterogeneous transition metal oxides to activate PMS. For a heterogeneous catalytic process, the degradation effect is positively correlated with the interaction between PMS and the catalyst. Thus a high specific surface area and porosity of the catalyst is desired (S. Yang et al., 2018).
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are porous crystalline polymers constructed by strong coordination bonds between metal ions and organic ligands (Furukawa, Cordova, O'Keeffe, & Yaghi, 2013). These materials are known for their interesting properties such as extremely high specific surface areas, tunable porous sizes and low densities (H. C. Zhou, Long, & Yaghi, 2012). Based on these advantages, MOFs have shown high potential for applications in organic pollutant degradation. Lin et al. prepared ZIF-76 as a heterogeneous catalyst and degraded rhodamine B (RB) by activating PMS in water (K.-Y. A. Lin & Chang, 2015). Recently, bimetallic MOFs were prepared by adding a second metal to the frameworks, and these bimetallic MOFs have been shown to exhibit enhanced catalytic activity compared with that of single-metallic MOFs (L. J. Wang et al., 2014; X. Zhang et al., 2019). Sun et al. synthesized bimetallic Fe/Mn-MOFs and used them as Fenton-like catalysts to degrade phenol. Furthermore, the catalytic performances of the bimetallic Fe/Mn-MOFs was higher than that of the original Fe-MOFs (Sun et al., 2017). The catalytic properties of bimetallic MOFs are improved due to the generation of defects in the MOFs when a second metal is added. Additionally, the cooperative effect between the different metals in bimetallic MOFs is also an important factor in promoting catalytic properties (X. Yang & Xu, 2017). Compared with other bimetallic MOFs, bimetallic Co/Fe MOFs (Co/Fe bi-MOFs) exhibit more effective catalytic activity (Hu et al., 2018). However, Co/Fe bi-MOFs easily aggregate because of their high surface energy. Thus, it is necessary to disperse Co/Fe bi-MOFs on carriers to relieve agglomeration.
Cellulose is one of the most abundant organic materials in nature; it is inexpensive and demonstrates excellent properties, namely, its large specific area, sustainability and biocompatibility (Fan et al., 2020). Moreover, as a new nanomaterial, cellulose nanofibres (CNFs) have attracted extensive attention. Owing to its abundant functional groups, large aspect ratio and specific surface area, CNF is often used as a green reinforcement material (Nechyporchuk, Belgacem, & Bras, 2016; Zhu et al., 2018). Recently, CNFs were also reported as substrates to prepare hybrid fibrous materials (Matsumoto & Kitaoka, 2016). Zhou et al. synthesized four CNF $@$ MOF nanopapers by growing different MOFs on the surface of CNFs and then applying them to separate volatile organic compounds (VOCs) from air (S. Zhou, Strømme, & Xu, 2019). On the one hand, the agglomeration of MOFs can be relieved and endowed with hydrophilicity, biodegradability and other features when MOFs are loaded on cellulose materials compared with those of pure MOFs (Nadar, Vaidya, Maurya, & Rathod, 2019). On the other hand, composite materials are also beneficial for separating the catalyst from the reaction solution during degradation. For the preparation of Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF, Co/Fe bi-MOFs were synthesized by solvothermal method. However, the cross-linking structure of cellulose fibers can be easily damaged at the same temperature and pressure. Therefore, vacuum filtration is a more suitable method to load Co/Fe bi-MOFs on CNF membranes than the in-situ method.
Inspired by these findings, a rod-like Co/Fe bi-MOFs were synthesized by a facile one-step solvothermal method and loaded on CNFs by vacuum filtration, which was then applied in SR-AOPs for the catalytic degradation of TBBPA. During the vacuum filtration process, MOF crystals adhered to CNFs because CNFs have abundant carboxy groups and a large specific surface area. The CNFs formed high density of hydrogen bonds and physical entanglements after vacuum filtration because of the large aspect ratio and inter-fibril interactions of CNFs (Zhu et al., 2018). Therefore, the cellulose network of the Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF composite membrane could maintain its original shape after multiple catalytic experiments. In addition, the catalyst loading amount could be precisely controlled by the vacuum filtration method. In the Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF/PMS systems, the TBBPA degradation efficiency was affected by the PMS dose, catalyst loading amount, and initial $\mathrm{pH}$ of the solutions. Furthermore, the various affecting parameters were optimized to improve the TBBPA removal efficiency and almost $100\%$ of TBBPA was degraded under optimal conditions. The stability and reusability of Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF are satisfactory.
Finally, the possible oxidation mechanism and TBBPA intermediates were elucidated in detail.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Chemicals
$\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ , terephthalic acid $\mathrm{\langleH_{2}B D C)}$ and N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) were purchased from Damao Chemical Reagent Factory. iron(III) acetylacetonate $({\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{acac})_{3}) $ ) and tetrabromobisphenol A (TBBPA) were obtained from Aladdin Co., China. Potassium peroxymonosulfate $2\mathrm{KHSO}_{5}{\cdot}\mathrm{KHSO}_{4}{\cdot}\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ , PMS) was obtained from Macklin Inc., China. Anhydrous methanol (MeOH) were obtained from Concord Technology Co., Ltd. The scavengers anhydrous ethanol (EtOH) and tert-butanol (TBA) were obtained from Ante Food Co., Ltd. The cellulose nanofiber (CNF) was obtained from Tianjin University of Science and Technology. All chemical reagents were directly used without further purification and all solutions were prepared using ultra-pure water.
## 2.2. Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF membrane preparation
Co/Fe bi-MOFs were synthesized according to Yang S et al. with a slight modification (S. Yang et al., 2018) and the synthesis was illustrated in Scheme 1. In a typical process, Fe(acac)3 (1 mmol) and $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ 1 $(0.5\ \mathrm{mmol})$ were dissolved in $25~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF and $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of EtOH solution under vigorous stirring. Subsequently, $1.67~\mathrm{mmol}$ terephthalic acid was slowly added to the premixed solution. Then, the obtained colloidal solution was further stirred with ultrasonic for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ to dissolve all the solid matter. The resultant mixture was transferred into a $100\mathrm{-mL}$ Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave, sealed and maintained at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling naturally, the product was collected by centrifugation, washed with EtOH three times and vacuum dried at $65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. Thereafter, Co/Fe bi-MOFs $\mathrm{20~mg)}$ ) were dispersed in $2\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{CNF}$ solution under stirring. Finally, the well dispersed colloidal solution was filtered through a $0.22\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ filter to form a membrane. Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF membranes were obtained by freeze drying to liberate the solvent.
