## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization
The SEM image in Fig. 1(a) showed that the morphology of Co/Fe bi-MOFs and consisted of uniform rod-like micro-scale particles. Moreover, Fig. 1(a) also presented that the Co/Fe bi-MOFs easily aggregated without any support materials. The SEM image (Fig. 1(b)) of the prepared Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF showed that these rod-like MOFs were uniformly distributed in or on the CNF membrane. This phenomenon demonstrated that CNF effectively ameliorated the aggregation problem when was used as the Co/Fe bi-MOFs support. Furthermore, the XRD patterns of Co/Fe bi-MOFs, CNF and Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF (Fig. 2) showed a series of sharp peaks. The main diffraction peaks of Co/Fe bi-MOFs were located at $(2\theta=)11.4^{\circ},16.4^{\circ},17.4^{\circ}$ , $18.3^{\circ}$ , $20.5^{\circ}$ and $32.7^{\circ}$ , which demonstrated that the ordered Co/Fe bi-MOFs samples were well crystallized (Hu et al., 2018; H. Wang, Yin, Li, Chen, & Wang, 2014). Additionally, the CNF exhibited three main diffraction peaks positioned at $(2\theta=)$ $15.2^{\circ}$ , $16.3^{\circ}$ and $22.7^{\circ}$ , which demonstrated the cellulose Ι structure of the CNF samples (French, 2013). The XRD pattern of Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF showed similar peaks as those of Co/Fe bi-MOFs and CNF, indicating that Co/Fe bi-MOFs were successfully loaded on the CNF membrane.
To further elucidate the structure and functional groups of the Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF, Raman spectra were obtained for analysis. As presented in Fig. 3(a), the organic ligands in MOFs showed typical Raman spectra in the high frequency region. The peak at $1606~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ was assigned to the ν $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C})$ modes of the benzene ring. The bands at 1439 and $1140~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ were attributed to the - $_{(\mathrm{C=O)O-}}$ group and - $_\mathrm{(C=O)OH}$ group of the organic ligands, respectively. The peak at $858~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ was assigned to the C-H vibration. The peaks at 436 and $633~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ in the low frequency region were considered to possibly be characteristic of vibrational modes involving the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ species in Co/Fe bi-MOFs (H. Yang et al., 2017; Zhao, Chen, Peng, Wang, & Zhao, 2017).
FTIR spectra were collected using samples from 500 to $4000\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to confirm Co and Fe connections to organic ligands and the presence of functional groups in the MOFs (Fig. 3(b)). The characteristic peaks at 3313 ,1597 and $1386~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ represented the -OH groups on cellulose, H-O-H groups and carboxyl groups $scriptstyle(\mathrm{O=C-OH})$ , respectively. The stretching vibration peak at $1654~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ was attributed to the coordination of the carbonyl group $(\mathrm{C=O})$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ and DMF. The peak at 1500 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was mainly the stretching vibration of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ in the benzene ring. The peak at $1116\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was related to the C-O stretching vibration in the organic ligands and CNF. The absence of sharp peaks near $752\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponded to the C-H bending vibrations of benzene. The low intensity peaks at 542 and $690~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ were characteristic of the Fe-O stretching vibration in FeOOH and the ${\mathrm{Co-O}}$ stretching vibration, respectively (K. Y. Lin & Chen, 2016; X. Lin et al., 2016; Song et al., 2014; H. Wang et al., 2014; Zhao et al., 2017). These results showed that the metal ions were successfully coordinated with the organic ligands to form MOF, which were then loaded onto CNF membranes.
The chemical valence and surface composition of Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF were also characterized by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The peaks of C, O, Co and Fe were found in the XPS survey spectra of Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF (Fig. S2). In the XPS spectra of O 1s (Fig. 4(a)), two peaks at 531.8 and $533.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ were found which corresponded to Fe-O/Co-O and C-O, respectively. In the XPS spectra of C 1s (Fig. 4(b)), three major peaks were observed. They implied that there were different functional groups of carbon, including $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C}}$ (284.8 eV), C-O (286.6 eV) and C=O/O=C-OH $288.2\:\mathrm{eV}$ ) (Leng et al., 2017; Li et al., 2019).
The thermal stability of Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF was evaluated by thermogravimetric analysis. As shown in Fig. 5, the first weight loss from room temperature to $210^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was ascribed to the removal of the bound water and adsorbed solvent molecules in the sample. The second stage from $210^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $525^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was related to the decomposition of the MOFs and CNF, losing approximately $54.44\%$ of the total weight. In the last stage, the TG curve showed a slight change. The results showed that the Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF sample had good thermal stability.
## 3.2. Catalytic degradation of TBBPA
Fig 6(a) showed the catalytic degradation of TBBPA under different conditions. Since PMS produced free radicals by itself in water (Wu et al., 2019), approximately $28\%$ of TBBPA was degraded by only PMS in $30~\mathrm{min}$ . To elimination the adsorbed particles on the porous structure of the composite membrane, a Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF membrane was added to the reaction solution without PMS. Approximately $15\%$ of TBBPA was adsorbed by the composite membrane in $30\ \mathrm{\min}$ . Clearly, it was negligible for the Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF membrane or PMS alone to remove TBBPA after $30~\mathrm{min}$ . In contrast, the removal rate increased significantly to $100\%$ within 30 min when the composite membrane and PMS were added simultaneously to the TBBPA solution. Therefore, this phenomenon indicated that the simultaneous existence of the Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF membrane and PMS was an effective way to degrade TBBPA. Fig 6(b) presented the catalytic performance under different PMS doses. The degradation rate of TBBPA was positively correlated with PMS dose when the concentration of PMS increased from 0.025 to $0.2\ \mathrm{mmol/L}$ . With increasing PMS concentration from $0.025\ \mathrm{\mmol/L}$ $(\mathbf{k}{=}0.0253\ \mathrm{\min}^{-1}$ , Fig. S3a) to 0.2 mmol/L $(\mathrm{k{=}0.7640~m i n^{-1}}$ , Fig. S3a), the degradation rate of TBBPA significantly increased from $56\%$ to $100\%$ within $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The catalyst activated more free radicals from PMS when increasing the PMS concentration. Therefore, the TBBPA degradation rate was significantly improved within the above range. Fig 6(c) exhibited the catalytic performance under different catalyst loading amounts. It was advantageous for TBBPA degradation to increase the catalysts dose. Because the number of active sites were increased in the reaction system when the catalyst dose was increased from 0.025 to $0.2~\mathrm{g/L}$ , the rate constant was increased from $0.0784~\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ to $0.2001\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ (Fig. S3b), and the degradation rate was also improved. Hence, the optimum catalyst dose in this paper was $0.2\ \mathrm{g/L}$ . Moreover, the pH value was a non-negligible factor in AOPs. The effects of $\mathrm{pH}$ on the reaction were evaluated by adjusting the initial $\mathrm{\pH}$ value. However, the solubility of TBBPA in non-alkaline aqueous solution is poor. Thus, the minimum pH $\mathrm{(pH_{0})}$ was 7.5 in the paper. TBBPA degradation (Fig. S4) showed a slight change at $\mathrm{pH}$ values of 7.5 and 8.5. Although the TBBPA degradation capacity decreased more when the $\mathrm{pH}_{0}$ values was increased to 10, the degradation rate of TBBPA remained at about $60\%$ within $30~\mathrm{min}$ . This phenomenon indicated that the composite membrane had good catalytic capacity over a wide range of pH values. Fig 6(d) showed the degradation effect of the composite membrane after four cycles. The TBBPA degradation effect of the composite membrane decreased after the first degradation cycle because a small amount of the MOFs could be rinsed.
However, the degradation rate of TBBPA was still $60\%$ after four cycles. It suggested that Co/Fe bi-MOFs /CNF membrane had good stability.
3.3. Proposed activation mechanism and degradation pathway
Some papers have reported that $\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{\cdot-}$ and OH· are produced by activating PMS with different transition metals (Ghanbari & Moradi, 2017; Oh et al., 2016). It was essential to illuminate the activation mechanism to explore the change in the transition metal valence state during catalysis. There were XPS spectra of $\mathrm{Co}~2\mathrm{p}$ on the ${\mathrm{Co/Fe}}$ bi-MOFs/CNF surface before (Fig. 7(a)) and after (Fig. 7(b)) catalysis. Then the activation mechanism of PMS by Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF was further explored. Fig. 7(a) presented two peaks positioned at 779.1 and $783.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ representing ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ in the B-site, respectively (S. Yang et al., 2019). The relative areas of those peaks were $20\%$ and $12\%$ before catalysis, respectively. However, Fig. 7(b) showed that the relative areas of the peaks mentioned above were $6\%$ and $26\%$ , respectively. This phenomenon indicated that the catalytic reaction was initialized on the Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF surface. During the activation process, ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ possibly provided the electrons to produce free radicals (S. Yang et al., 2018). Therefore, the content of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ further increased from $12\%$ to $26\%$ . ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ also accepted the electrons from $\mathrm{HSO5^{-}}$ and was reduced to ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ in the catalytic reaction. So the above transformation maintained the electron/charge balance on the surface of Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF. Moreover, the redox cycle of $\mathbf{Co}$ (II-III-II) was proven during the PMS activation process. Meanwhile, in the XPS spectra of Fe 2p (Fig. 7(c) and 7(d)) on the same sample, five major peaks were observed before and after catalysis. The characteristic peaks positioned at 709.4 ,710.0, 711.9, 724.1 and $714.8~\mathrm{eV}$ represented the $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , Fe $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2},\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ,  Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ , and satellite peaks, respectively (Ali-Löytty et al., 2016). This indicated the presence of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ cations on the Co/Fe bi-MOFs/CNF surface. Furthermore, the relative areas of the $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ peaks changed slightly after the catalytic reaction. This revealed that an Fe (II-III-II) redox cycle was also existed during the catalytic reaction.
