To identify the intermediates of TBBPA during the degradation process, the degradation intermediates were analyzed by LC-MS/MS (Table S1, Fig. S5). Based on the experimental results and literature work (Ding, Zhua, Wang, & Tang, 2013; Y. Ji et al., 2016; Peng et al., 2017), the proposed TBBPA degradation pathways was illustrated in Fig. 9. First, radicals oxidized TBBPA, and one electron was transferred to the organics to produce the TBBPA radical cation $\mathrm{(TBBPA^{\bullet+}}$ ). Then, two different routes of radicals induced degradation (Ι) the cleavage of the central carbon of TBBPA•+ or (II) the substitution of the Br atom by hydroxylation and debromination. Three dibrominated products (A, B, D) were obtained by these two routes in the initial degradation of TBBPA. Next, the E and H compounds were produced by oxidizing the methyl group of the initial intermediates. Additionally, a number of initial intermediates also underwent hydroxylation and debromination to produce monobrominated intermediates compounds (F and G). Subsequently, these compounds were transformed into two short chain aliphatic acids (I and J) through the cleavage of the aromatic rings. Finally, a part of short chain aliphatic acids were further mineralized into $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ .
